WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:05.920
It's been a wild year in the Marvel Cinematic&nbsp;
Universe, with TV shows like "WandaVision,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.920 --> 00:00:10.960
"Loki," and "What If...?" paving the way for&nbsp;
the Multiverse to crack reality wide open&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:16.000
in "Spider-Man: No Way Home" and "Doctor Strange&nbsp;
in the Multiverse of Madness." Although the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:21.120
intention is to introduce alternate versions of&nbsp;
classic heroes and villains, there are tragic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:26.160
stories at the heart of these grand adventures --&nbsp;
and the "Doctor Strange" sequel is no different.

00:00:26.160 --> 00:00:31.120
The film sees the Scarlet Witch struggling to&nbsp;
cope after losing her children in "WandaVision"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.120 --> 00:00:35.200
and desperately wanting to find another&nbsp;
universe where she can be with them.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:39.680
In doing so, she dabbles in dark magic&nbsp;
while hunting the superpowered America&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.680 --> 00:00:43.600
Chavez, [CHA-vez] who has the ability&nbsp;
to punch her way between universes.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.720
Thankfully, the young hero has&nbsp;
Doctor Strange to protect her,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.720 --> 00:00:51.360
and their journey across the Multiverse takes&nbsp;
them to some wildly different universes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:55.200
Although most of their time is spent on Earth-838,&nbsp;
[eight three eight] which is where they meet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:55.200 --> 00:01:00.240
Professor X [ECKS], the rest of the Illuminati,&nbsp;
and a variant of Christine Palmer, they first&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:05.680
tumble through several other realities -- each&nbsp;
with its own unique visual style -- including a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.680 --> 00:01:11.520
prehistoric world, an underwater universe, and&nbsp;
even one where they're made entirely of paint.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:16.080
But the scene also hides a surprising&nbsp;
blink-and-you'll-miss-it "Spider-Man" Easter egg.

00:01:16.080 --> 00:01:20.080
Although Strange briefly name-drops Spider-Man&nbsp;
when discussing his experience with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:24.640
Multiverse, the film doesn't really address&nbsp;
the events of "Spider-Man: No Way Home."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:27.760
No, don't worry -- you didn't miss&nbsp;
a sneaky cameo from Tom Holland,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.760 --> 00:01:32.720
Tobey Maguire, or Andrew Garfield. Instead,&nbsp;
there's a surprising Easter egg in a sleek,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.720 --> 00:01:38.000
futuristic universe that shows up midway through&nbsp;
that psychedelic leap through alternate realities.

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:42.000
It's not exactly clear where they are within&nbsp;
that version of Earth, but it looks highly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.000 --> 00:01:46.880
advanced -- almost to the point where it has an&nbsp;
industrial feel to it. On the left-hand side of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:52.480
the screen, a drone flies past the two Multiversal&nbsp;
travelers -- and it has the exact same design as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:58.560
the Stark Industries drones used by Peter Parker&nbsp;
and Mysterio in "Spider-Man: Far from Home."

00:01:58.560 --> 00:02:03.680
The drones play a key part in Spidey's MCU&nbsp;
story because after Tony Stark's death, he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:03.680 --> 00:02:08.880
passes Peter the keys to artificial intelligence&nbsp;
called EDITH, which also grants him access to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:08.880 --> 00:02:13.680
satellite weapons and an army of drones. And&nbsp;
although Peter had learned to become a more&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.680 --> 00:02:18.640
responsible hero since "Spider-Man: Homecoming"&nbsp;
and even becomes an Avenger in "Avengers:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:24.240
Infinity War," Tony clearly wasn't thinking when&nbsp;
he decided to give a teenager that sort of power.

00:02:24.240 --> 00:02:28.960
Anyway, these drones ultimately cause chaos&nbsp;
and destruction at the hands of Mysterio,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.960 --> 00:02:34.320
even killing Mysterio himself. As we all know,&nbsp;
the villain posthumously leaks Peter's identity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.320 --> 00:02:38.720
to the world while blaming him for his death.&nbsp;
So it's a little surprising that one of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:43.520
universes shows the drone flying around a&nbsp;
futuristic world. What does it all mean?

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:48.080
We'll try not to get too excited here, but a&nbsp;
"Spider-Man" Easter egg in a futuristic setting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.080 --> 00:02:53.040
instantly makes us think of "Spider-Man&nbsp;
2099." As you may have guessed, his story&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.040 --> 00:02:58.160
is set at the end of the century, long after the&nbsp;
Avengers of old have all died or disappeared and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.160 --> 00:03:02.320
New York has become a dystopian nightmare&nbsp;
that wouldn't look out of place in "Blade&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.320 --> 00:03:07.760
Runner." Built on the ruins of the old city,&nbsp;
the bustling metropolis is called Nueva York,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.760 --> 00:03:12.960
and it is home to an incredibly powerful sinister&nbsp;
corporation called Alchemax [AL-kuh-MAX]. Miguel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:15.680
O'Hara [muh-GEL O-HAIR-uh] , a&nbsp;
geneticist working at the company,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:20.480
winds up having his genes rewritten with&nbsp;
a spider's DNA by accident -- and quickly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.480 --> 00:03:26.560
suits up as Spider-Man 2099 to fight the&nbsp;
injustices committed by his own employer.

00:03:26.560 --> 00:03:31.040
Now, the reference in "Multiverse of Madness"&nbsp;
probably means nothing in the grand scheme of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:36.160
things -- it's just an inventive look at another&nbsp;
universe that uses familiar imagery for fans to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:42.240
recognize -- but it would be very, very cool to&nbsp;
see Spider-Man 2099 in live action. Thankfully,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.240 --> 00:03:48.000
the futuristic web-slinger is already heading to&nbsp;
the big screen in 2023's "Spider-Man: Across the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:53.120
Spider-Verse" -- and Oscar Isaac is reprising the&nbsp;
role after voicing him in the post-credits scene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.120 --> 00:03:57.360
of "Spider-Man: Into the Spider-Verse."
But this sequence is another reason why&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:02.400
"Multiverse of Madness" is so refreshing: It&nbsp;
allows Marvel to really get creative with its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:07.520
visuals and its storytelling rather than sticking&nbsp;
to its often generic-looking action scenes.

